    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d906-a2">

                <pb facs="1050" n="970" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg970"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hardy to Mr. Lieberknecht.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1902-04-15"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April 15,
                    1902</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Inclosed please find returned the passport
                    application of Mrs. Bertha Knopf, and her expired passport, “canceled.”</p>
                <p>It appears that Mrs. Knopf married a native citizen of the United States, who has
                    since deserted her; that she has never been in the United States, and can make
                    no declaration of intention to go there. Under these circumstances, as at
                    present set forth, her application is refused. In the application made by her
                    husband in 1895 he declared himself to be a native citizen, and to have been
                    born in New York, December 2, 1870. In such a case the wife should make
                    application on the native form, the words “that I am the wife of a native” being
                    inserted in the proper place. I call your attention to this instruction of the
                    Department for your future guidance.</p>
                <p>Yours, very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Arthur S. Hardy.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
